// A quaternion Julia set: the points that stay bounded when `q -> q² + c` is applied over and over.
//
// **Every other surface in this gallery can be asked where a ray meets it.**  This one cannot.  There
// is no equation to solve, because whether a point belongs is settled by iterating until the answer
// either runs away or does not, and no closed form says when that happens.  What can be had instead
// is an *estimate* of how far the nearest surface is -- and a lower bound on that distance is enough
// to walk a ray by, taking strides through open space and shortening as it closes in.  It is the same
// marching a `sdf` uses, and it is the only reason this shape can be drawn at all.
//
// `c` is the shape itself.  Move it and you get a different object rather than the same one resized,
// which is why this surface takes numbers where a paraboloid or a saddle take none.
//
// A quaternion has four parts and space has three, so what is drawn is a **slice**, the fourth part of
// the point held at nought.  The whole object is four-dimensional and this is a section through it.
context { angles are degrees  no gamma }

camera { location [2.9, 2.3, -3.5]  look at [0, 0, 0]  field of view 38 }

point light { location [-6, 8, -7]  color [1, 0.96, 0.90] }
point light { location [5, 3, -4]   color [0.26, 0.30, 0.40] }

background linear Y bouncing gradient {
    [0, [0.06, 0.07, 0.11], 0.6, [0.14, 0.16, 0.24], 1, [0.22, 0.26, 0.36]]
}

julia {
    c [-0.2, 0.6, 0.2, 0]
    iterations 12
    material {
        pigment [0.82, 0.62, 0.34]
        specular 0.55  shininess 160
        ambient 0.10
    }
}
